00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.749 align:start position:0%
Francis Bacon captured the rawness of  the human condition and used a striking  palette to indulge his fascination with  the body his paintings often represented  doomed lovers or dwelled on death a new  exhibition at Paris's Pompidou Center  shows how the British 20th century  artists exploration of existence  intensified in his later years his goal  in his work it's to express life but  when you express life when you're  fascinated by life you have to deal with  death because life and death are mixed  together Bacon had a reputation for  being witty and a gambler a ba fever who  drew inspiration from post-war London's  nightlife by contrast his canvases  simmer with profound and at times  violent contemplation compete with the  with the things that's happening every  day in the world so I can't imagine why  people should think my work disturbing  the exhibition also focuses on Bacon's  love of literature we established a  relation between his literature interest  and is painted because we know that  bacon was a heavy Grieder and more and  more doing in time he talked about  literature  Francis Bacon is considered one of 20th  century Western arts most important and  influential figurative painters and is  more remarkable because he was never  formally trained it was actually here in  Paris that as a young man he decided to  become an artist on Bacon's first trip  to Paris in the 1920s a Picasso  exhibition inspired him to become an  artist he returned to the city often and  befriended famous French writers  including missionaries the link with the  with the French intellectuals is very  important in that way and when he moves  to Paris when it gets this studio and  will be like in 74 he starts to meet  more often lilies which is presenting  him to other poets like Jacques Pepin  and so on the 60 paintings on display  ban the last two decades of Bacon's life  and include his last known work it was  finished a few months before the 82 year  old artist died in Madrid in 1992 the  shadowy figure of a fading bull is full  of mystery and foreboding a sign perhaps  that Baker knew his own existence was  nearing its end  Natasha Butler al Jazeera Paris
 

